<InfoPage title="How we evaluate fitness gear." eyebrow="RESEARCH & TESTING" intro="A transparent process that distinguishes real hands-on tests from research-based comparisons.">
<h2>Two evaluation methods</h2><p><strong>Hands-on testing:</strong> Only used when the author actually handled and evaluated a product. The article should describe test conditions, duration and real observations, with original photographs when available.</p><p><strong>Research-based assessment:</strong> Draws on clearly cited manufacturer documentation, manuals, independent measurements and other verifiable information. Articles explicitly state they were not personally tested.</p>
<h2>What matters by category</h2><ul><li><strong>Dumbbells:</strong> Weight range, increment spacing, adjustment mechanism, storage footprint, safety and warranty.</li><li><strong>Yoga mats:</strong> Thickness, grip, materials, usable dimensions, cleaning and portability.</li><li><strong>Treadmills:</strong> Belt size, verified motor ratings, stability, folding dimensions, noise and safety features.</li><li><strong>Fitness trackers:</strong> Supported sensors, battery life, app compatibility and connectivity.</li><li><strong>Recovery gear:</strong> Dimensions, material, ease of cleaning and manufacturer warnings.</li></ul>
<h2>Ratings and prices</h2><p>We do not publish fabricated scores, customer testimonials, live prices or discount claims. Ratings should only appear when a documented and repeatable evaluation process supports them. Prices require a trustworthy update process.</p>
<h2>How articles are updated</h2><p>We revise published details when new specifications, availability or relevant evidence changes. A modified date is shown only when a substantive edit has actually occurred.</p>
</InfoPage>
